10	 * {@code '+'} keeps its country code as given; otherwise country code {@code +61} is
11	 * assumed and a single leading {@code '0'} is dropped from the national digits. The
12	 * result must contain 8 to 15 digits after the {@code '+'}; anything else is not a
13	 * valid E.164 number.
14	 *
15	 * <p>Where the country code is known its national-number length is enforced exactly:
16	 * {@code +61} (Australia) requires 9 national digits and {@code +1} (NANP) requires 10.
17	 * A number whose national part is the wrong length for its country code is not a valid
18	 * E.164 number. Country codes not listed here are only bound by the general 8-to-15
19	 * total-digit rule.
20	 *
21	 * <p>For example {@code "0412 345 678"} becomes {@code "+61412345678"} and
22	 * {@code "+64 21 123 456"} becomes {@code "+6421123456"}.
23	 */
24	final class TelephoneE164 {
